  "slug": "gridui-09-one-border-width-control-three-paths-currently-disagree",
  "title": "gridui-09: One border-width control — three paths currently disagree",
  "discussedAt": "",
  "contextReady": false,
  "contextReadyReason": "",
  "summary": "User-reported 2026-06-23: with the global border set to 0, toasts/modals/bracketed panes still show a hairline, and raising it adds no border to a plain Pane.",
  "description": "Rescued from PLAN.md 2026-07-26 before that file was deleted. Reported by the user 2026-06-23,\nanalysed then, never tracked. The analysis below is theirs and the line numbers are from that date —\nre-verify before building.\n\n═══ WHAT THE USER SAW ═══\n- With the global BORDER set to **0**: notifications (`Toast`), `Modal`, and `Bracketed` panes\n  **still show a hairline border**, and the showcase demo panes keep a 2px border.\n- Raising BORDER **adds no border** to the default `DASHBOARD` `Pane`.\n\n═══ WHY — THREE BORDER PATHS THAT DISAGREE ═══\n1. **`theme.border_width`-driven** — `Button`, `Input`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `Tag`, `Card`.\n   `PaintCx::border()` (component.rs, ~line 567 as of 2026-06-23) returns `None` at width 0.\n   **This one is already correct: 0 means none.**\n2. **`PaintCx::bracket_frame`** — used by `Toast` (toast.rs ~332), `Modal` (modal.rs ~257) and\n   `Pane::Bracketed` (pane.rs ~143). At `border_width == 0` it deliberately falls back to a solid\n   hairline (`BRACKET_HAIRLINE_WIDTH` / `BRACKET_HAIRLINE_ALPHA`) so the container \"stays framed\".\n   **That fallback is why Toast/Modal/Bracketed keep a border at 0.**\n3. **Hardcoded literals in the showcase** — `heca-renderer/examples/showcase.rs` (~762, 771, 793,\n   819): `.border(theme.accent, 2.0)`. Ignores the global control entirely.\n\nPlus: a `Bordered` `Pane` draws `style.visual.border`, which comes from an explicit\n`.border(color, width)` call. A pane that never called it (the DASHBOARD pane, showcase.rs ~635)\nshows no border whatever the global setting says.\n\nNOTE 2026-07-26: border now lives at `style.visual.border` after the Layout/Visual split\n(F003/P017/T1, commit 8718718). Re-check the line numbers above against current code.\n\n═══ THE PROPOSED MODEL (designed 2026-06-23, NOT built — the user chose \"plan, don't code\") ═══\n- `theme.border_width` becomes the ONE global control.\n- **`border_width == 0` means no border anywhere.** That means dropping the `bracket_frame`\n  hairline fallback AND updating the test that currently asserts it:\n  `bracket_frame_zero_border_is_a_uniform_hairline_not_broken_corners` in\n  `heca-grid-ui/tests/phase_a.rs`.\n- A container's border **width always comes from `theme.border_width`**; a widget picks only the\n  colour. A `Bordered` pane with no explicit colour uses the theme border colour.\n- The showcase reads `theme.border_width` instead of the literal `2.0`.\n\n═══ THE OPEN DECISION — SETTLE WITH THE USER BEFORE BUILDING ═══\nDoes an explicit `.border(color, width)` still override the global width, or is width **always**\ntheme-driven? Both are defensible and the answer changes the widget API.\nThis ties into the planned config options (memory `border-style-config-options`:\n`sidebar_border_style` / `pane_border_style`).\n\n═══ SEPARATE FROM ═══\nThe bracket reticle fix on branch `fix/bracket-frame-render`. Do not conflate them.\n\n═══ VERIFICATION ═══\nBorders are visible. Green tests are not verification: build the showcase, set the global width to\n0 and then to something large, and have the USER confirm every container agrees.",
